","output":"Method: HEAD
Path: /
Query: none
Host: codinghub.in
Safe: yes
Idempotent: yes"}]},"quiz":[{"type":"concept","question":"What is the job of DNS when a browser opens a website?","options":["Translating a domain name into an IP address","Compressing HTTP headers","Encrypting traffic between browser and server","Rendering HTML into pixels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DNS maps names such as codinghub.in to IP addresses. Encryption is TLS's job and rendering is the browser engine's."},{"type":"concept","question":"Which HTTP method is idempotent but NOT safe?","options":["PUT","GET","POST","HEAD"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PUT changes state (not safe) but repeating the same PUT leaves the same state (idempotent). GET and HEAD are both; POST is neither."},{"type":"scenario","question":"A logged-in student calls an admin-only endpoint. The server recognises the student's valid token. Which status code fits best?","options":["400 Bad Request","403 Forbidden","401 Unauthorized","404 Not Found"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The user is authenticated but not permitted, which is 403. 401 is for missing or invalid credentials."},{"type":"scenario","question":"A mobile client's POST /api/orders times out and the client retries automatically. Two orders appear. What is the most robust fix?","options":["Switch the endpoint to GET","Send an idempotency key so the server can detect the duplicate","Increase the client timeout to five minutes","Return 500 for every second request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"POST is not idempotent. An idempotency key lets the server return the first result for a repeated request instead of creating a duplicate."},{"type":"output","question":"What does this print?","code":"const res = new Response('{}', { status: 503 });
console.log(res.ok, Math.floor(res.status / 100));","options":["false 5","true 503","true 5","false 503"],"answer":0,"explanation":"res.ok is true only for 200–299, so it is false. Math.floor(503 / 100) gives the status class 5 (server error)."}]}

{"day":2,"title":"Modern JavaScript (ES6+): Scope, Closures and Array Methods","module":"Web and JavaScript foundations","level":"Beginner","objectives":["Explain the differences between var, let and const, including hoisting and the temporal dead zone.","Define a closure and use one to keep private state.","Use destructuring, spread/rest, template literals, optional chaining and nullish coalescing correctly.","Transform data with map, filter, reduce and sort without mutating the source.","Explain how arrow functions treat this differently from regular functions."],"definition":"ES6 (ECMAScript 2015) and later versions added block-scoped variables, arrow functions, classes, modules, destructuring, spread syntax, promises and many array and object helpers to JavaScript. A closure is a function bundled with the variables of the scope it was created in, so it can read and update those variables even after the outer function has returned.","theory":[{"heading":"var, let and const","body":"var is function-scoped and hoisted with the value undefined, so it leaks out of blocks such as if and for. let and const are block-scoped; they are hoisted too, but reading them before the declaration line throws a ReferenceError (the temporal dead zone). const prevents reassignment of the binding, not mutation of the object it points to. Modern code uses const by default and let when the variable must be reassigned.","points":["const user = {}; user.name = 'Asha' is allowed; user = {} is not.","for (let i ...) creates a fresh i per iteration; for (var i ...) shares one i.","Avoid var in new code."]},{"heading":"Closures","body":"Every function remembers the lexical environment where it was defined. When an inner function is returned or passed as a callback, it still has access to the outer variables. This is how JavaScript creates private state without classes, and it is the mechanism behind React hooks, event handlers, debounce utilities and module patterns. A classic bug is a stale closure: a callback captures an old value and keeps using it after the value has changed.","points":["Private state: a counter factory exposes increment() but hides count.","Function factories: makeMultiplier(3) returns a function that multiplies by 3.","Stale closures appear in React effects and timers that capture old state."]},{"heading":"Modern syntax you will use daily","body":"Destructuring pulls values out of objects and arrays; spread copies or merges them; rest collects remaining items. Optional chaining (a?.b) stops at null or undefined instead of throwing, and nullish coalescing (x ?? fallback) uses the fallback only for null or undefined, unlike || which also replaces 0, '' and false. These features are everywhere in React props and API response handling.","points":["const { name, role = 'student' } = user; default applies only when the value is undefined.","const next = { ...state, count: state.count + 1 } makes a shallow copy.","res.data?.choices?.[0]?.message ?? 'No reply' guards a deeply nested API field.","Template literals: `Hello ${name}` with embedded expressions."]},{"heading":"Array methods and immutability","body":"map transforms each item, filter keeps items that pass a test, reduce folds a list into one value, find returns the first match, and some/every test conditions. They return new values instead of modifying the input, which is exactly what React state updates require. Note that sort and reverse mutate the original array; use [...arr].sort() or the newer toSorted() to stay immutable.","points":["map → same length, transformed items.","filter → same or fewer items, unchanged.","reduce(fn, initial) → any shape: a number, an object, a grouped map.","sort compares as strings by default: [10, 9, 1].sort() gives [1, 10, 9]."]},{"heading":"Arrow functions and this","body":"A regular function gets this from how it is called (obj.method() sets this to obj). An arrow function has no own this: it uses this from the surrounding scope. That makes arrows ideal for callbacks inside methods, but wrong as object methods that need the object as this. Arrows also have no arguments object and cannot be used with new.","points":["Use arrows for callbacks: items.map((x) => x * 2).","Use regular methods when you need the object as this."]}],"example":{"title":"A closure-based usage tracker","body":"This factory keeps a per-user token count private. Callers can add usage and read the total, but cannot change count directly because it only exists inside the closure.","code":{"language":"javascript","source":"function createUsageTracker(limit) {
  let used = 0;                       // private: only the inner functions can see it
  return {
    add(tokens) {
      if (used + tokens > limit) return false;
      used += tokens;
      return true;
    },
    remaining: () => limit - used,
  };
}

const tracker = createUsageTracker(1000);
tracker.add(400);
tracker.add(700);                     // rejected: would exceed the limit
console.log(tracker.remaining());     // 600
console.log(tracker.used);            // undefined"}},"use_cases":[{"title":"Shaping API responses","description":"map and filter turn a raw list of records into the exact props a React component needs."},{"title":"Aggregations in the browser","description":"reduce groups items by category or sums prices in a cart without a library."},{"title":"Encapsulated utilities","description":"Closures power debounce, throttle, memoize and per-user counters."},{"title":"Safe access to nested data","description":"Optional chaining and ?? handle missing fields in LLM or third-party API responses without crashes."}],"demonstration":{"description":"Group chat messages by role and count tokens per role with one reduce call, then show that the input array was not modified.","code":{"language":"javascript","source":"const messages = [
  { role: 'user', tokens: 12 },
  { role: 'assistant', tokens: 140 },
  { role: 'user', tokens: 9 },
];
const totals = messages.reduce((acc, { role, tokens }) => {
  acc[role] = (acc[role] ?? 0) + tokens;
  return acc;
}, {});
console.log(totals);
console.log(messages.length);"},"output":"{ user: 21, assistant: 140 }
3"},"takeaways":["Prefer const, use let for reassignment, avoid var.","A closure keeps access to its outer variables; it enables private state and is behind React hooks.","?? falls back only for null/undefined; || also replaces 0, '' and false.","map, filter and reduce return new values; sort and reverse mutate unless you copy first.","Spread makes shallow copies: nested objects are still shared.","Arrow functions take this from the surrounding scope."],"interview":[{"question":"What is the difference between var, let and const?","answer":"var is function-scoped and initialised to undefined when hoisted. let and const are block-scoped and sit in the temporal dead zone until declared. const cannot be reassigned, though an object it holds can still be mutated.","explanation":"Mention the loop example (var shares one variable across iterations) and that const is about the binding, not deep immutability."},{"question":"What is a closure? Give a practical example.","answer":"A closure is a function that remembers variables from the scope where it was created. Example: a counter factory that returns increment and get functions sharing a private count, or a debounce function that remembers its timer ID between calls.","explanation":"A concrete use (private state, debounce, React hooks) is what separates a strong answer from a memorised definition."},{"question":"What does this print, and why? for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) setTimeout(() => console.log(i));","answer":"It prints 3, 3, 3. var creates one function-scoped i shared by all callbacks, and the callbacks run after the loop has finished. Changing var to let prints 0, 1, 2 because let creates a new binding per iteration.","explanation":"This classic question tests scope, closures and the event loop together. Explain both the cause and the fix."},{"question":"When would you use reduce instead of map or filter?","answer":"Use reduce when the result has a different shape from the input, for example a total, an object keyed by category, or a lookup map. Use map for one-to-one transforms and filter for selecting items, because they are clearer.","explanation":"Interviewers like hearing that readability matters: chaining filter then map is often clearer than one clever reduce."},{"question":"Is the spread operator a deep copy? Why does this matter in React?","answer":"No. Spread copies only the top level; nested objects and arrays are shared references. In React, mutating a nested object after spreading changes the old state too, which can cause missed re-renders or bugs. Copy every level you change, or use structuredClone or a helper like Immer.","explanation":"Connecting shallow copies to React's reference-equality checks shows you understand why immutability matters, not just the rule."}],"practice":{"kind":"coding","title":"Course leaderboard with map, filter and reduce","difficulty":"Basic","objective":"Practise the parse → validate → group → aggregate pipeline using array methods and immutable sorting.","statement":"Each input line is 'name,course,score'. A record is valid when name and course are non-empty and score is a number from 0 to 100. Group valid records by course and, for each course in alphabetical order, print the average score (one decimal place), the top student (highest score; ties go to the alphabetically first name) and the number of students. Finally print how many lines were skipped as invalid. If there are no valid records, print 'No valid records' before the skipped count.","language":"javascript","input_format":"Zero or more non-empty lines 'name,course,score'. Surrounding spaces around each field are ignored.","output_format":"One line per course: '<course>: avg=<x.x> top=<name> (<score>) count=<n>', then 'Skipped: <k>'.","example":{"input":"Asha,react,88
Ravi,react,92
Meena,node,75
Kiran,node,75
Zoya,react,70
","output":"node: avg=75.0 top=Kiran (75) count=2
react: avg=83.3 top=Ravi (92) count=3
Skipped: 0"},"constraints":["Scores may be decimals; print them as JavaScript prints numbers (75, 99.5).","Course names are compared with plain string ordering.","Do not mutate the parsed array when sorting; copy it first."],"starter":"const rows = stdin.split('\n').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);

// TODO: parse each row into { name, course, score }
// TODO: filter valid rows, group with reduce, print each course
","solution":"const rows = stdin.split('\n').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);

const parsed = rows.map((line) => {
  const [name = '', course = '', raw = ''] = line.split(',').map((s) => s.trim());
  const score = raw === '' ? NaN : Number(raw);
  return { name, course, score };
});

const isValid = ({ name, course, score }) =>
  name !== '' && course !== '' && Number.isFinite(score) && score >= 0 && score <= 100;
const valid = parsed.filter(isValid);

const byCourse = valid.reduce((acc, r) => {
  (acc[r.course] = acc[r.course] || []).push(r);
  return acc;
}, {});

const courses = Object.keys(byCourse).sort();
if (courses.length === 0) console.log('No valid records');
for (const course of courses) {
  const list = byCourse[course];
  const avg = list.reduce((sum, r) => sum + r.score, 0) / list.length;
  const top = [...list].sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score || (a.name < b.name ? -1 : 1))[0];
  console.log(`${course}: avg=${avg.toFixed(1)} top=${top.name} (${top.score}) count=${list.length}`);
}
console.log(`Skipped: ${parsed.length - valid.length}`);
","explanation":["Split into trimmed, non-empty lines and map each to an object with destructuring defaults for missing fields.","Convert the score with Number, treating an empty string as NaN so it fails validation.","filter keeps valid records; reduce groups them into an object keyed by course.","For each course (sorted keys), compute the average with reduce and find the top student by sorting a copy with a two-level comparator.","The skipped count is the difference between parsed and valid lengths."],"tests":[{"input":"Asha,react,88
Ravi,react,92
Meena,node,75
Kiran,node,75
Zoya,react,70
","output":"node: avg=75.0 top=Kiran (75) count=2
react: avg=83.3 top=Ravi (92) count=3
Skipped: 0"},{"input":"Dev,python,101
Neha,python,64
Om,python,abc
Ira,ai,99.5
","output":"ai: avg=99.5 top=Ira (99.5) count=1
python: avg=64.0 top=Neha (64) count=1
Skipped: 2"},{"input":"Ana,js,-5
","output":"No valid records
Skipped: 1"},{"input":" Sam , react , 50 
Tia,react,50
,react,90
Uma,,80
","output":"react: avg=50.0 top=Sam (50) count=2
Skipped: 2"}]},"quiz":[{"type":"output","question":"What does this code print?","code":"const fns = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) fns.push(() => i);
console.log(fns.map((f) => f()).join(','));","options":["3,3,3","0,1,2","undefined,undefined,undefined","0,0,0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"let creates a new binding of i for each iteration, so each closure captures its own value."},{"type":"output","question":"What is logged?","code":"const count = 0;
console.log(count || 10, count ?? 10);","options":["0 10","10 0","0 0","10 10"],"answer":1,"explanation":"|| treats 0 as falsy and returns 10; ?? only falls back for null or undefined, so it returns 0."},{"type":"code","question":"Which line updates a React-style state object immutably, adding a tag to its tags array?","options":["const next = { ...state, tags: [...state.tags, 'ai'] };","state.tags.push('ai'); const next = state;","const next = state; next.tags = next.tags.concat('ai');","const next = { ...state }; next.tags.push('ai');"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Only the first copies both the object and the nested array. The last one shares the original tags array, so push mutates old state."},{"type":"concept","question":"Why can an arrow function be a poor choice for an object method that uses this?","options":["Arrow functions take this from the surrounding scope, not from the object","Arrow functions cannot read outer variables","Arrow functions cannot return objects","Arrow functions always run asynchronously"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Arrows do not bind their own this, so inside an object literal this refers to the outer scope rather than the object."},{"type":"output","question":"What does [10, 9, 1].sort() return?","options":["[1, 9, 10]","[1, 10, 9]","[10, 9, 1]","[9, 10, 1]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a comparator, sort converts items to strings: '1' < '10' < '9'. Use sort((a, b) => a - b) for numbers."}]}

{"day":3,"title":"Asynchronous JavaScript: Event Loop, Promises and async/await","module":"Web and JavaScript foundations","level":"Beginner","objectives":["Explain how a single-threaded JavaScript runtime handles slow I/O without blocking.","Describe the call stack, the microtask queue and the task (macrotask) queue, and the order they run in.","Use promises and async/await, including proper error handling with try/catch.","Predict the output order of code mixing synchronous logs, promises and timers.","Recognise common async bugs such as missing await and accidental sequential requests."],"definition":"JavaScript runs your code on a single thread, so long operations such as network requests, timers and file reads are handed to the environment (the browser or Node.js) and their results come back later as queued callbacks. The event loop runs those callbacks when the call stack is empty. A Promise represents a value that will be available in the future, and async/await is syntax that lets you write promise-based code in a readable, top-to-bottom style.","theory":[{"heading":"Why asynchronous code exists","body":"If JavaScript waited synchronously for a 3-second LLM response, the browser could not repaint or respond to clicks and a Node.js server could not serve any other request. Instead, the runtime starts the operation, keeps running other code, and calls your code back when the result is ready. This non-blocking model is why one Node.js process can handle thousands of concurrent connections that mostly wait on I/O.","points":["I/O-bound work (network, disk, database) fits the async model well.","CPU-bound work (image processing, large loops) still blocks the thread; move it to a Worker or worker_threads.","await pauses only the current async function, not the whole program."]},{"heading":"Call stack, microtasks and tasks","body":"The event loop repeats one cycle: run a task to completion, then run every microtask in the microtask queue (including microtasks added while draining), then let the browser render if needed, then take the next task. Promise callbacks (then, catch, finally, code after await) and queueMicrotask are microtasks. setTimeout, setInterval, I/O callbacks and UI events are tasks. So a resolved promise's callback always runs before a setTimeout(fn, 0) scheduled at the same moment.","points":["Synchronous code first, until the stack is empty.","Then all microtasks, in FIFO order.","Then one macrotask, followed again by all microtasks.","setTimeout(fn, 0) means 'as a later task', not 'immediately'."]},{"heading":"Promises","body":"A promise is pending, then settles once as fulfilled (with a value) or rejected (with a reason); after settling it never changes. then returns a new promise, which is what makes chaining work: whatever a then callback returns becomes the next value, and a thrown error skips to the nearest catch. Always return or await promises you create; a promise that is created but not awaited can fail silently as an unhandled rejection.","points":["new Promise((resolve, reject) => ...) wraps callback-style APIs.","Promise.resolve(v) and Promise.reject(e) create settled promises.","finally runs on both outcomes, useful for hiding a loading spinner."]},{"heading":"async/await and error handling","body":"An async function always returns a promise. Inside it, await pauses until the awaited promise settles, then resumes with its value or throws its rejection reason, so ordinary try/catch works. A frequent performance bug is awaiting independent requests one after another; start them together and await Promise.all instead. A frequent correctness bug is forgetting await, which returns a pending promise and lets errors escape your try/catch.","points":["Sequential: const a = await getA(); const b = await getB(); takes timeA + timeB.","Parallel: const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getA(), getB()]); takes max(timeA, timeB).","fetch only rejects on network failure; check res.ok for 4xx/5xx."]}],"example":{"title":"Fetching a chat reply with proper error handling","body":"This helper awaits the response, converts HTTP error statuses into thrown errors (fetch does not do that by itself) and always clears the loading state in finally.","code":{"language":"javascript","source":"async function sendMessage(text, setLoading) {
  setLoading(true);
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/chat', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ message: text }),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed with ${res.status}`);
    const data = await res.json();
    return data.reply;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err.message);
    return 'Sorry, something went wrong.';
  } finally {
    setLoading(false);
  }
}"}},"use_cases":[{"title":"Calling APIs from React","description":"Every data fetch, form submission and LLM request in the front end is asynchronous."},{"title":"Parallel data loading","description":"A dashboard loads profile, courses and notifications at once with Promise.all."},{"title":"Non-blocking servers","description":"An Express handler awaits a database query and an LLM call while Node.js keeps serving other users."},{"title":"Responsive UIs","description":"Long work is split or moved off the main thread so typing and scrolling stay smooth."}],"demonstration":{"description":"Predict the order before running. Synchronous logs come first, then the microtask from the promise, then the timer task.","code":{"language":"javascript","source":"console.log('1: script start');
setTimeout(() => console.log('4: timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3: promise'));
(async () => {
  console.log('2: inside async, before await');
  await null;
  console.log('3b: after await');
})();
console.log('2b: script end');"},"output":"1: script start
2: inside async, before await
2b: script end
3: promise
3b: after await
4: timeout"},"takeaways":["JavaScript is single-threaded; the event loop runs queued callbacks when the stack is empty.","Microtasks (promise callbacks, code after await) run before the next macrotask (timers, I/O, events).","An async function always returns a promise; await unwraps it or throws its rejection.","Use Promise.all for independent requests instead of sequential awaits.","fetch resolves on HTTP errors; check res.ok yourself.","CPU-heavy work blocks the event loop; offload it."],"interview":[{"question":"Explain the event loop.","answer":"JavaScript executes code on one call stack. Async operations are handled by the runtime, and their callbacks are queued. When the stack is empty, the event loop runs all pending microtasks (promise reactions), then takes the next macrotask (timer, I/O, UI event), runs it, drains microtasks again, and repeats.","explanation":"The key detail interviewers check is the microtask-before-macrotask rule. A small example (promise logs before setTimeout 0) makes the answer convincing."},{"question":"What is the difference between a promise and a callback?","answer":"A callback is a function you pass to be called later; nesting many leads to 'callback hell' and inconsistent error handling. A promise is an object representing a future result that settles once, can be chained, composes with Promise.all, and propagates errors to a single catch.","explanation":"Mention the trust guarantees: a promise settles only once, and its callbacks always run asynchronously."},{"question":"What does async/await do under the hood?","answer":"An async function returns a promise. Each await suspends the function and schedules its continuation as a microtask once the awaited promise settles; the result is returned or the rejection is thrown at the await point. It is syntax over promises, not a new threading model.","explanation":"Say clearly that await does not block the thread: other code keeps running while the function is suspended."},{"question":"Your page makes three independent API calls with three awaits in a row and feels slow. What would you change?","answer":"Start all three requests first and await them together with Promise.all, so total time is the slowest call rather than the sum. If one failure should not cancel the others, use Promise.allSettled and handle each result.","explanation":"This is a very common practical question. Mentioning allSettled for partial failure shows depth."},{"question":"Why might a try/catch around an async call not catch an error?","answer":"If the promise is not awaited (or returned) inside the try block, the function continues and the try block exits before the rejection happens, so the rejection becomes unhandled. Also, fetch does not reject on 404 or 500, so there is nothing to catch unless you check res.ok and throw.","explanation":"Covering both the missing-await bug and the fetch status behaviour shows real debugging experience."}],"practice":{"kind":"coding","title":"Simulate the event loop","difficulty":"Basic","objective":"Apply the event-loop rules (synchronous first, then all microtasks, then one macrotask at a time) to predict execution order.","statement":"Each input line is a statement in a script: '<type> <label>' or '<type> <label> then <type> <label>'. The type is sync, micro or macro. Processing a statement means: sync runs immediately, micro is added to the microtask queue, macro is added to the macrotask queue. When a task runs it prints its label; if it has a 'then' part, that child statement is processed at that moment. After the whole script, drain the microtask queue completely (including microtasks added while draining), then repeatedly run one macrotask followed by draining microtasks, until both queues are empty. Print labels in the order they run.","language":"javascript","input_format":"One statement per line. Labels contain no spaces.","output_format":"One label per line in execution order.","example":{"input":"sync start
macro timeout
micro promise
sync end
","output":"start
end
promise
timeout"},"constraints":["At most one 'then' per line (children have no further children).","Queues are FIFO.","1 to 50 statements."],"starter":"const statements = stdin.split('\n').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
const micro = [];
const macro = [];

// TODO: parse each line into { type, label, child }
// TODO: dispatch(task) and run(task), then drain the queues
","solution":"const lines = stdin.split('\n').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
const micro = [];
const macro = [];
const out = [];

function parse(line) {
  const [head, tail] = line.split(' then ');
  const [type, label] = head.trim().split(/\s+/);
  return { type, label, child: tail ? parse(tail) : null };
}

function run(task) {
  out.push(task.label);
  if (task.child) dispatch(task.child);
}

function dispatch(task) {
  if (task.type === 'sync') run(task);
  else if (task.type === 'micro') micro.push(task);
  else macro.push(task);
}

function drainMicrotasks() {
  while (micro.length) run(micro.shift());
}

lines.map(parse).forEach(dispatch);
drainMicrotasks();
while (macro.length) {
  run(macro.shift());
  drainMicrotasks();
}
console.log(out.join('\n'));
","explanation":["Parse each line into a task object; the part after ' then ' becomes a child task.","dispatch runs sync tasks immediately and pushes micro and macro tasks onto FIFO queues.","run records the label and dispatches the child, so a microtask can schedule a macrotask and vice versa.","After the script, drain microtasks fully; the while loop also picks up microtasks added during draining.","Then run exactly one macrotask at a time, draining microtasks after each, which mirrors the real event loop."],"tests":[{"input":"sync start
macro timeout
micro promise
sync end
","output":"start
end
promise
timeout"},{"input":"macro t1 then micro m-in-t1
macro t2
micro m1 then macro t3
sync s
","output":"s
m1
t1
m-in-t1
t2
t3"},{"input":"micro a then micro b
micro c
sync d then micro e
","output":"d
a
c
e
b"},{"input":"sync x then sync y
macro z
","output":"x
y
z"},{"input":"macro only
","output":"only"}]},"quiz":[{"type":"output","question":"In what order are the letters logged?","code":"console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'));
console.log('D');","options":["A C D B","A D C B","A B C D","A D B C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Synchronous logs A and D run first, then the promise microtask C, then the timer task B."},{"type":"concept","question":"What does an async function return when you call it?","options":["Always a Promise","The value of its return statement, synchronously","undefined until the function finishes","A generator object"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An async function always returns a promise that resolves with the returned value or rejects with the thrown error."},{"type":"scenario","question":"A page awaits getProfile(), then getCourses(), then getNotifications(). Each takes about 300 ms and none depends on another. What is the best improvement?","options":["Start all three and await Promise.all","Call them inside a for loop with await","Wrap each call in setTimeout","Use var instead of const for the results"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent requests should run concurrently; Promise.all reduces total time from about 900 ms to about 300 ms."},{"type":"concept","question":"A promise's executor calls resolve('ok') and then reject(new Error('x')). What is the promise's final state?","options":["Rejected with Error('x')","Fulfilled with 'ok'","Pending forever","It throws a TypeError"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A promise settles only once. The first call wins; the later reject is ignored."},{"type":"output","question":"What is printed?","code":"async function f() {
  console.log(1);
  await null;
  console.log(2);
}
f();
console.log(3);","options":["1 3 2","2 1 3","1 2 3","3 1 2"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The async function runs synchronously until the first await, so 1 prints first. The rest is a microtask, so 3 prints before 2."}]}

{"slug":"javascript-event-loop","title":"JavaScript event loop: tasks, microtasks and a responsive page","category":"Languages","difficulty":"Intermediate","summary":"Predict promise and timer ordering, explain why async does not make CPU work parallel, and split expensive UI work safely.","sections":[{"id":"execution","title":"Separate the call stack from scheduled work","paragraphs":["JavaScript executes a piece of synchronous code on the current call stack. An asynchronous API can arrange later work without keeping that stack frame running. A promise callback does not interrupt the current synchronous function. It becomes eligible after the current work completes and the runtime reaches the relevant microtask checkpoint.","In a browser, timers and many event callbacks are tasks. Promise reactions and queueMicrotask callbacks use the microtask queue. The browser ordinarily drains pending microtasks before moving to another task or a rendering opportunity. This ordering explains common examples, but timing-sensitive rendering behavior should be tested in the actual runtime."]},{"id":"example","title":"Predict an ordering example","paragraphs":["A and D are synchronous. B is a promise reaction and C is a timer callback, so the expected browser ordering is A, D, B, C. A timer delay of zero means no earlier than a later eligible task; it does not mean immediate execution or an exact scheduling deadline."],"language":"javascript","code":"console.log('A');
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('B'));
setTimeout(() => console.log('C'), 0);
console.log('D');
// Browser output: A, D, B, C"},{"id":"cpu","title":"Async does not move CPU work off the main thread","paragraphs":["An async function runs synchronously until it reaches an await that yields. A long sorting loop before that point still blocks input and rendering. Awaiting an already resolved promise schedules continuation as a microtask; repeatedly doing this can keep the microtask queue busy without giving the browser a useful rendering opportunity.","For substantial computation, consider a worker and account for the cost of copying or transferring data. For incremental UI work, split the job into bounded chunks using an appropriate task scheduling mechanism supported by the target browsers. Start with measurement: a worker adds complexity and does not help when the bottleneck is a slow network response."]},{"id":"failure","title":"Handle ordering, errors and cancellation together","paragraphs":["A search interface can receive the result for an older query after a newer query. Debouncing reduces requests but does not establish response ordering. Abort a previous request when possible and retain an identity or active flag so stale responses cannot replace current results. Handle rejected promises and distinguish cancellation from a real loading failure.","Use the performance panel to inspect long tasks and input responsiveness. Test a fast response, a slow response, rapid query changes and navigation away during a request. Explain which state is authoritative rather than assuming that the latest response to arrive belongs to the latest user action."]}],"exercise":"Add a second promise callback from inside B and a second timer from inside C. Predict the order, run it in a browser console, and explain any difference between prediction and observation."}

{"slug":"oop-composition","title":"OOP design: composition and a small notification boundary","category":"OOP","difficulty":"Beginner","summary":"Separate a business action from message delivery using a small interface, then test behavior without a deep class hierarchy.","sections":[{"id":"responsibilities","title":"Identify responsibilities before creating classes","paragraphs":["Consider an enrollment service that validates a course choice, persists an enrollment and sends a notification. These are different reasons for change. Course rules change with the product; persistence changes with the database; delivery changes with the mail provider. Putting all three into a growing superclass makes independent changes harder to reason about.","Composition means constructing an object with the collaborators it needs. The enrollment service can depend on a small notification boundary rather than an SMTP client or a large inheritance tree. The interface should describe the business need, such as enrollment_received, without exposing provider-specific options to every caller."]},{"id":"example","title":"A small boundary in code","paragraphs":["Python protocols describe the required behavior without requiring implementations to inherit from one base class. The example returns the persistence result and lets delivery be implemented independently. In a durable application, enqueue the notification after the transaction commits rather than sending it before the enrollment exists."],"language":"python","code":"from typing import Protocol

class Notifier(Protocol):
    def enrollment_received(self, enrollment_id: int) -> None: ...

class EnrollmentService:
    def __init__(self, repository, notifier: Notifier):
        self.repository = repository
        self.notifier = notifier

    def enroll(self, student_id, course_id):
        enrollment = self.repository.create(student_id, course_id)
        self.notifier.enrollment_received(enrollment.id)
        return enrollment"},{"id":"tradeoffs","title":"When inheritance still helps","paragraphs":["Inheritance is useful when a subtype can safely fulfill the contract of its parent. A subclass that rejects operations callers reasonably expect from the parent violates substitutability. Do not choose inheritance only to reuse a few lines; extracting a small helper or composing a collaborator may express the relationship more honestly.","Likewise, an interface for every one-line function is not automatically good design. Introduce a boundary where behavior varies, side effects need isolation or a dependency must be replaceable. Keep the public contract smaller than the implementation details it hides, and avoid passing an entire application context when two dependencies are sufficient."]},{"id":"testing","title":"Test the contract and the failure paths","paragraphs":["Use a fake repository to supply a real domain-shaped enrollment and a recording notifier to capture the enrollment identifier. Assert that successful persistence leads to the expected notification request. Separately test repository failure, duplicate enrollment and notification failure. The service must not report a durable enrollment failure merely because an optional email timed out after commit.","In an interview, explain who owns retries and idempotency. A durable outbox makes a notification request recoverable after a process restart. The interface should make that boundary clear: enqueueing a message is different from proving it was delivered to an inbox."]}],"exercise":"Add an SMS notifier without changing enrollment rules. Then explain whether both channels should be called synchronously, queued independently or selected by user preference."}

{"day":1,"title":"JVM, JRE and JDK: How Java Runs and Manages Memory","module":"Core Java foundations","level":"Beginner","objectives":["Distinguish the JDK, JRE and JVM and explain what each one contains.","Trace the path from a .java file to running machine code (compile, class loading, interpretation, JIT).","Describe the JVM memory areas: heap, thread stacks, metaspace and the PC register.","Explain how garbage collection decides which objects can be freed, including objects in reference cycles.","Diagnose StackOverflowError and OutOfMemoryError from their causes."],"definition":"The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) is the runtime engine that loads compiled bytecode (.class files), verifies it and executes it, translating it to native machine code. The JRE (Java Runtime Environment) is the JVM plus the core class libraries needed to run programs, and the JDK (Java Development Kit) is the JRE plus development tools such as the javac compiler, jshell, javadoc and jdb. The JVM also manages memory automatically: objects live on a shared heap and are reclaimed by the garbage collector when nothing can reach them.","theory":[{"heading":"JDK vs JRE vs JVM","body":"Think of the three as nested boxes. The JVM executes bytecode; the JRE wraps the JVM with the standard library (java.lang, java.util, java.io and so on); the JDK wraps the JRE with tools for building programs. Since Java 11, Oracle and most vendors no longer ship a separate JRE download; you install a JDK, and tools such as jlink can build a trimmed runtime image for deployment.","points":["JVM: class loader, bytecode verifier, interpreter, JIT compiler, garbage collector.","JRE: JVM + core libraries; enough to run, not to compile.","JDK: JRE + javac, jar, jshell, javadoc, jdb, jconsole, jcmd and more.","The JVM is platform-specific; bytecode is platform-independent. That is the meaning of 'write once, run anywhere'."]},{"heading":"From source code to running program","body":"javac compiles Main.java into Main.class containing bytecode, an instruction set for the JVM rather than for a real CPU. At runtime the class loader subsystem loads classes on demand using parent delegation (Bootstrap, then Platform, then Application class loader), the verifier checks that the bytecode is safe, and the execution engine runs it. HotSpot starts by interpreting bytecode and profiles which methods run often; hot methods are compiled to optimised native code by the JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler. This is why Java services get faster after a short warm-up.","points":["Loading → Linking (verify, prepare, resolve) → Initialization (static blocks run).","Parent delegation stops an application from replacing core classes such as java.lang.String.","Tiered compilation: C1 compiles quickly, C2 compiles hot code aggressively."]},{"heading":"JVM memory areas","body":"Every thread gets its own stack, made of frames: one frame per method call, holding local variables, parameters and partial results. Primitive locals live directly in the frame; for objects the frame holds only a reference, while the object itself is created on the heap, which all threads share. Class metadata (method bytecode, field layouts, the runtime constant pool) lives in Metaspace, which uses native memory and replaced PermGen in Java 8. Each thread also has a program counter register and a native method stack.","points":["Stack: per thread, LIFO frames, freed automatically when a method returns, fast.","Heap: shared, holds all objects and arrays, managed by the garbage collector.","Metaspace: class metadata in native memory (tune with -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize).","Deep or infinite recursion fills the stack → StackOverflowError.","Too many live objects for -Xmx → OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space."]},{"heading":"How garbage collection works","body":"The collector does not count references. It starts from GC roots (local variables in active stack frames, static fields, active threads, JNI references) and marks every object it can reach by following references. Anything not marked is garbage, even if unreachable objects still point at each other in a cycle. Most collectors are generational because most objects die young: new objects go to the young generation (Eden plus two survivor spaces), survivors of several minor collections are promoted to the old generation, and the old generation is collected less often.","points":["Mark: find live objects from the roots. Sweep/compact: reclaim the rest and reduce fragmentation.","Minor GC cleans the young generation; major/full GC cleans the old generation too.","G1 is the default collector since Java 9; ZGC and Shenandoah target very short pauses on large heaps.","System.gc() is only a hint; never rely on it, and finalize() is deprecated."]},{"heading":"Why this matters in interviews and production","body":"Memory questions test whether you understand what your code costs. A Spring Boot service that caches everything in a static map leaks memory because static fields are GC roots; a recursive tree walk on deep data can overflow the stack; a container started without heap limits may be killed by the orchestrator. Knowing the memory model lets you read a heap dump, choose -Xms/-Xmx sensibly and explain a production incident clearly."}],"example":{"title":"Where each value lives","body":"In the method below, the int count and the reference variable name are stored in the stack frame of main. The String object "Asha" lives in the heap (in the string pool), and the Student object is allocated on the heap with its fields inside it. When main returns, the frame is popped; the Student object becomes unreachable and is eligible for garbage collection.","code":{"language":"java","source":"public class Main {
    static class Student {
        String name;   // reference stored inside the heap object
        int marks;     // primitive stored inside the heap object
        Student(String name, int marks) { this.name = name; this.marks = marks; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int count = 1;                       // stack: primitive value
        String name = "Asha";                // stack: reference -> pooled String on heap
        Student s = new Student(name, 91);   // stack: reference -> Student on heap
        System.out.println(s.name + " " + s.marks + " " + count);
        s = null;                            // Student object now unreachable -> GC eligible
    }
}"}},"use_cases":[{"title":"Sizing a Spring Boot container","description":"Setting -Xmx or -XX:MaxRAMPercentage so the heap fits inside a Docker memory limit and leaves room for metaspace and thread stacks."},{"title":"Diagnosing a memory leak","description":"Taking a heap dump with jcmd or on OutOfMemoryError and finding a static collection or cache that keeps objects reachable."},{"title":"Reducing latency spikes","description":"Reading GC logs, then choosing G1 pause targets or ZGC for services where long stop-the-world pauses hurt users."},{"title":"Fixing a StackOverflowError","description":"Replacing deep recursion (for example walking a large category tree) with an explicit stack or iteration."},{"title":"Portable deployments","description":"Shipping the same JAR to Windows developer laptops and Linux servers because only the JVM is platform-specific."}],"demonstration":{"description":"Compile and run a program from the command line, then trigger both memory errors on purpose to see the difference between running out of stack and running out of heap.","code":{"language":"text","source":"$ javac Main.java          # JDK tool: produces Main.class (bytecode)
$ java Main                # JVM loads Main.class and runs main()
Asha 91 1

// recurse() { recurse(); }  with no base case
$ java Recurse
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.StackOverflowError

// keep adding 1 MB arrays to a static List
$ java -Xmx64m Hoard
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space"},"output":"The first error comes from one thread's stack filling with frames; the second comes from the shared heap filling with objects that stay reachable through a static field, so the GC cannot free them."},"takeaways":["JDK ⊃ JRE ⊃ JVM: compile with the JDK, run on the JVM.","Bytecode is portable; the JVM is the platform-specific part, and the JIT makes hot code fast.","Primitive locals and references live on the thread's stack; objects and arrays live on the shared heap.","GC frees objects that are unreachable from GC roots, so reference cycles are collected too.","Static fields are GC roots: an ever-growing static collection is the classic Java memory leak.","StackOverflowError means too-deep calls; OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space means too many live objects."],"interview":[{"question":"What is the difference between the JDK, the JRE and the JVM?","answer":"The JVM executes bytecode. The JRE is the JVM plus the standard class libraries needed to run applications. The JDK is the JRE plus development tools such as javac, jar, jshell and debuggers, so you need the JDK to compile and the JVM to run.","explanation":"Describe them as nested layers and add that the JVM is platform-specific while bytecode is platform-independent. Mentioning that modern Java ships only JDKs (with jlink for slim runtimes) shows current knowledge."},{"question":"Is Java compiled or interpreted?","answer":"Both. javac compiles source to bytecode ahead of time. At runtime the JVM first interprets the bytecode and then the JIT compiler compiles frequently executed methods into native machine code, using profiling data to optimise them.","explanation":"A strong answer names the JIT, explains warm-up and mentions tiered compilation (C1/C2). That shows you know why Java performance improves after start-up."},{"question":"Explain the difference between stack and heap memory in Java.","answer":"Each thread has its own stack containing frames for method calls, with local primitives and object references; it is freed automatically when methods return. The heap is shared by all threads and stores every object and array; it is managed by the garbage collector. A too-deep call chain causes StackOverflowError, while too many live objects cause OutOfMemoryError.","explanation":"Interviewers look for thread ownership (stack per thread, heap shared), what lives where (references vs objects) and the matching error for each. Noting that heap objects need synchronization when shared is a bonus."},{"question":"How does the garbage collector decide an object can be collected? What about two objects that reference each other?","answer":"An object is eligible when it is not reachable from any GC root: local variables of live frames, static fields, active threads and JNI references. The collector marks everything reachable from the roots and reclaims the rest. Two objects that only reference each other are unreachable from the roots, so both are collected; Java does not use reference counting.","explanation":"The cycle question checks that you understand reachability rather than counting references. Mention mark, sweep and compact phases for depth."},{"question":"What is generational garbage collection, and which collector does modern Java use by default?","answer":"Most objects die young, so the heap is split into a young generation (Eden and survivor spaces) collected often and cheaply, and an old generation for long-lived objects collected less often. G1 has been the default since Java 9; it divides the heap into regions and aims for a configurable pause target. ZGC and Shenandoah offer very low pauses for large heaps.","explanation":"Linking the weak generational hypothesis to the design shows reasoning, not memorisation. Naming tuning flags such as -Xmx and -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis adds practical credibility."},{"question":"Your Spring Boot service's memory grows until it crashes with OutOfMemoryError. How would you investigate?","answer":"Enable -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError (or take a dump with jcmd GC.heap_dump), analyse it in a tool such as Eclipse MAT or VisualVM, and look at the dominator tree and paths to GC roots. Common culprits are static or unbounded caches, listeners never removed, ThreadLocals in thread pools and loading whole tables into memory. Fix the retention, bound caches, then verify with GC logs.","explanation":"This scenario tests production maturity. Good answers give a method (dump, analyse, find the root path, fix, verify) instead of just 'increase -Xmx'."}],"practice":{"kind":"coding","title":"Mark-and-sweep garbage collection simulator","difficulty":"Basic","objective":"Model how the JVM decides which objects are garbage: everything reachable from GC roots survives, everything else, including cycles, is collected.","statement":"You are given the objects on a heap, the GC roots and the references between objects. Perform the mark phase by following references from every root, then report which objects are reachable and which would be collected by the sweep phase. Objects that reference each other but cannot be reached from a root must be collected.","language":"java","input_format":"Line 1: object names separated by spaces.
Line 2: the root objects separated by spaces, or a single '-' if there are no roots.
Each remaining line: two names 'A B' meaning object A holds a reference to object B. There may be no reference lines.","output_format":"Two lines:
