long l = (long)o;","options":["l becomes 5","l becomes 0","Compile-time error","InvalidCastException at run time"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A boxed value can only be unboxed to its exact type (int here). Use (long)(int)o or Convert.ToInt64(o)."},{"type":"concept","question":"Where is an int field of a class instance stored?","options":["In a separate boxed object","In the CPU cache only","Always on the stack","Inline inside the object on the managed heap"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Value-type fields are stored inline in their containing object. Because the object is on the heap, so is the field."},{"type":"scenario","question":"A SQL column DiscountPercent allows NULL, meaning 'no discount configured', which differs from 0. Which C# property type models it best?","options":["int","object","decimal?","string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Nullable<decimal> distinguishes 'no value' (null) from a real 0 and keeps strong typing. object would require boxing and casts."}]}

{"day":3,"title":"Strings, StringBuilder and Method Parameters (ref, out, in, params)","module":".NET and C# fundamentals","level":"Beginner","objectives":["Explain why strings are immutable and what that means for performance.","Decide when to use StringBuilder instead of string concatenation.","Use ref, out, in and params parameters correctly and explain their differences.","Apply the TryParse-style 'Try' pattern with out parameters.","Compare strings correctly using ordinal and culture-aware comparisons."],"definition":"A C# string is an immutable sequence of UTF-16 characters: every operation that seems to change it (ToUpper, Replace, +) returns a new string. StringBuilder is a mutable buffer for building text efficiently in loops. Method parameters are passed by value by default; the ref, out and in modifiers pass a variable by reference, and params lets a method accept a variable number of arguments.","theory":[{"heading":"String immutability and interning","body":"Once created, a string object never changes. s.ToUpper() returns a new string and leaves s untouched, which is a classic interview trap. Immutability makes strings thread-safe and safe to use as dictionary keys, and it lets the runtime intern literals: identical string literals in an assembly share one object. The cost is that repeated concatenation in a loop creates a new string each time, copying all previous characters, which is O(n^2) work for n appends.","points":["s = s + x creates a new string; the old one becomes garbage.","Literals and compile-time constant expressions are interned automatically.","string.Concat, string.Join and interpolation are fine for a fixed, small number of parts.","Use string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace to validate input."]},{"heading":"StringBuilder","body":"StringBuilder keeps an internal resizable character buffer. Append writes into the buffer and only ToString() creates the final string, so building text in a loop becomes linear. Use it when the number of appends is large or unknown, such as generating CSV rows, reports or SQL in a loop. For two or three pieces, plain concatenation or interpolation is clearer and the compiler already optimises it.","points":["Append, AppendLine, Insert, Remove, Replace and Clear modify the buffer in place.","Pass an initial capacity when you know the approximate size to avoid resizes.","StringBuilder is not thread-safe; do not share one across threads."]},{"heading":"ref, out, in and params","body":"ref passes a variable by reference: the method can read and replace the caller's variable, and the caller must initialise it first. out is also by reference but the method must assign it before returning, and the caller need not initialise it; it is used to return extra values, as in int.TryParse(text, out int n). in passes by reference read-only, which avoids copying large structs. params lets callers pass a comma-separated list that arrives as an array, as in string.Join(",", a, b, c).","points":["ref: two-way, must be initialised by the caller.","out: one-way out, must be assigned by the method.","in: read-only reference, useful for large readonly structs.","params must be the last parameter, and only one is allowed.","Optional parameters and named arguments: Send(to, subject: "Hi", urgent: true)."]},{"heading":"The Try pattern","body":"The .NET convention for operations that can fail in expected ways is a Try method that returns bool and gives the result through an out parameter: int.TryParse, Dictionary.TryGetValue, Enum.TryParse. It avoids using exceptions for normal control flow, which is both faster and clearer. Use the throwing version (int.Parse) only when invalid input is truly exceptional.","points":["if (int.TryParse(input, out int qty)) { ... } else { show validation error }","Exceptions are expensive and should not drive ordinary branching."]},{"heading":"Comparing strings correctly","body":"== on strings compares contents ordinally (character codes). For case-insensitive checks use string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) rather than a.ToLower() == b.ToLower(), which allocates two strings and can behave unexpectedly in some cultures (the Turkish 'I' problem). Use culture-aware comparison only for text displayed and sorted for humans; use ordinal comparison for identifiers, keys, file paths and protocol values.","points":["StringComparison.Ordinal / OrdinalIgnoreCase for technical strings.","StringComparison.CurrentCulture for user-facing sorting.","new Dictionary<string, int>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase) gives case-insensitive keys."]}],"example":{"title":"Concatenation in a loop vs StringBuilder","body":"Both methods build the same text. The first allocates a new, longer string on every iteration; the second appends into one buffer and allocates the final string once.","code":{"language":"csharp","source":"using System;
using System.Text;

class Program
{
    static string Slow(int n)
    {
        string s = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) s += i + ",";   // n new strings
        return s;
    }

    static string Fast(int n)
    {
        var sb = new StringBuilder(n * 4);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sb.Append(i).Append(',');
        return sb.ToString();                          // one final string
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Slow(5)); // 0,1,2,3,4,
        Console.WriteLine(Fast(5) == Slow(5)); // True
    }
}"}},"use_cases":[{"title":"Report and CSV generation","description":"Build thousands of rows with StringBuilder before writing a file or HTTP response."},{"title":"Input validation","description":"Use int.TryParse and DateTime.TryParse on form or query-string values instead of catching exceptions."},{"title":"Case-insensitive lookups","description":"Compare HTTP header names, usernames or file extensions with OrdinalIgnoreCase."},{"title":"Helper APIs","description":"Expose params arrays for convenience methods such as Log(string format, params object[] args)."}],"demonstration":{"description":"Swap two integers with ref, divide with an out remainder and sum a params list.","code":{"language":"csharp","source":"static void Swap(ref int a, ref int b) { int t = a; a = b; b = t; }
static int Divide(int a, int b, out int remainder) { remainder = a % b; return a / b; }
static int Sum(params int[] values) { int s = 0; foreach (int v in values) s += v; return s; }

int x = 1, y = 2;
Swap(ref x, ref y);
Console.WriteLine(x + " " + y);
int q = Divide(17, 5, out int r);
Console.WriteLine(q + " r " + r);
Console.WriteLine(Sum(1, 2, 3, 4));"},"output":"2 1
3 r 2
10"},"takeaways":["Strings are immutable: methods like ToUpper return a new string.","Use StringBuilder for many or unknown numbers of appends; plain concatenation for a few.","ref needs an initialised variable; out must be assigned by the method; in is a read-only reference.","Prefer the Try pattern (TryParse, TryGetValue) over exceptions for expected failures.","Use ordinal comparisons for technical strings and culture-aware ones for display text."],"interview":[{"question":"Why are strings immutable in .NET?","answer":"Immutability makes strings thread-safe, safe as dictionary keys and hash codes, and allows interning so identical literals share memory. The trade-off is that every modification creates a new string.","explanation":"Give the benefits and the cost; then mention StringBuilder as the solution to the cost."},{"question":"When should you use StringBuilder?","answer":"When building a string from many pieces, especially in a loop where the count is large or unknown. For a handful of pieces, concatenation or interpolation is just as fast and more readable.","explanation":"Saying 'always use StringBuilder' is a red flag. Explaining the O(n^2) copying of repeated concatenation is a strong answer."},{"question":"What is the difference between ref and out?","answer":"Both pass a variable by reference. ref requires the caller to initialise the variable and lets the method read and change it. out does not require initialisation, but the method must assign it before returning; it is used to return additional values.","explanation":"Add in (read-only by reference) for completeness, and cite int.TryParse as the standard out example."},{"question":"How do you compare two strings case-insensitively, and why not use ToLower()?","answer":"Use string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase). ToLower() allocates new strings and is culture-sensitive, which can give wrong results (for example the Turkish dotted and dotless I).","explanation":"Mentioning the culture