Error: account exists for meena
Error: amount must be positive
meena balance: -1000
Error: insufficient funds"},{"input":"REPORT
","output":"No accounts"},{"input":"OPEN savings a
OPEN current b
DEPOSIT a 700
DEPOSIT b 700
OPEN current c
DEPOSIT c 900
REPORT
","output":"Opened Savings account for a
Opened Current account for b
a balance: 700
b balance: 700
Opened Current account for c
c balance: 900
Current c: 900
Savings a: 700
Current b: 700"}]},"quiz":[{"type":"concept","question":"Which method does print(obj) call first?","options":["__repr__","__str__","__format__ only","__print__"],"answer":1,"explanation":"print uses str(obj), which calls __str__ and falls back to __repr__ if __str__ is not defined."},{"type":"scenario","question":"You need User.from_dict(data) to build instances, and subclasses such as AdminUser should get AdminUser objects from it. How should it be defined?","options":["As a @staticmethod returning User(...)","As a module-level function","As a @classmethod returning cls(...)","As an instance method"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A classmethod receives the class it was called on, so cls(...) creates the right subclass."},{"type":"output","question":"What does this code with a shared class attribute print?","code":"class Cart:
    items = []
a, b = Cart(), Cart()
a.items.append('pen')
print(len(b.items))","options":["AttributeError","None","1","0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"items is a class attribute shared by every instance, so appending through a is visible through b."},{"type":"concept","question":"A class defines __eq__ but not __hash__. What happens when you put an instance in a set?","options":["The set silently ignores it","It works using id()","It works using __eq__ only","TypeError: unhashable type"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Defining __eq__ sets __hash__ to None, which makes instances unhashable."},{"type":"code","question":"class Admin(LoginRequiredMixin, ListView) calls dispatch(). Which class's dispatch runs first?","options":["ListView","View","object","LoginRequiredMixin"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The MRO lists LoginRequiredMixin before ListView, so its dispatch runs first and calls super().dispatch()."}]}

{"day":1,"title":"Scope, Closures and Functions: The JavaScript Behind React","module":"JavaScript essentials for React","level":"Beginner","objectives":["Explain lexical scope and the difference between var, let and const.","Define a closure and describe what a function 'remembers' after its outer function returns.","Use closures to create private state, the same idea React hooks rely on.","Recognise the stale-closure bug and explain why it appears in React event handlers and effects.","Answer common closure interview questions, including the classic loop-with-setTimeout puzzle."],"definition":"Scope decides where a variable can be read. JavaScript uses lexical scope: a function can see the variables that existed where it was written, not where it is called. A closure is a function together with the variables from its surrounding scope that it keeps alive, so it can still read and update them after the outer function has finished running.","theory":[{"heading":"Lexical scope and block scope","body":"Every function creates a new scope, and with let and const every block ({ ... }) does too. When JavaScript looks up a name it searches the current scope, then the enclosing one, and so on up to the global scope; this chain is fixed by where the code is written. var is function-scoped and hoisted with the value undefined, which caused many bugs; let and const are block-scoped and sit in a 'temporal dead zone' until their declaration runs. Modern React code uses const by default and let only when a variable must be reassigned.","points":["var: function scope, hoisted as undefined, can be redeclared.","let: block scope, can be reassigned, cannot be read before its declaration.","const: block scope, cannot be reassigned (the object it points to can still be mutated).","Lookup goes outward through enclosing scopes, never inward."]},{"heading":"What a closure is","body":"When an inner function refers to a variable of an outer function, the engine keeps that variable alive as long as the inner function exists. The inner function 'closes over' the variable. This is not a copy of the value: it is a live reference to the binding, so updates made by one closure are visible to every other closure that shares the same scope.","points":["Closures are created every time a function is created, not only when it is returned.","Each call to the outer function creates a fresh set of variables, so two counters do not share state.","Closures enable data privacy: the variable cannot be reached except through the returned functions."]},{"heading":"Why closures matter in React","body":"A function component is just a function that runs on every render. Event handlers, effect callbacks and callbacks passed to children are closures created during that render, so they see the props and state of that specific render. This is why React feels predictable, and also why a handler created in an old render can read an old value: the 'stale closure' problem. useState itself is built on the same idea: React stores state outside your function and hands each render a snapshot.","points":["Each render has its own props, state and handlers (a snapshot).","A stale closure reads values from an earlier render, often inside setInterval or an effect with missing dependencies.","Fixes: functional updates (setCount(c => c + 1)), correct dependency arrays, or a ref for the latest value."]},{"heading":"Arrow functions and this","body":"Arrow functions do not have their own this, arguments or prototype; they use this from the enclosing scope. In class components developers bound methods or used arrow class fields to keep this pointing at the component. Function components avoid this entirely, which is one reason closures replaced this-based patterns in modern React.","points":["Arrow functions are concise and ideal for callbacks such as items.map(item => ...).","Regular functions get this from how they are called; arrows take it from where they are defined."]},{"heading":"Costs and pitfalls","body":"Closures keep their captured variables in memory, so a long-lived closure (for example a listener that is never removed) can keep large objects alive and cause leaks. In React, creating new closures on every render is normally cheap; it only matters when a new function identity breaks memoisation of a child, which is what useCallback addresses later in the course.","points":["Remove event listeners and clear timers in cleanup functions.","New function identity each render is fine unless a memoised child or a dependency array depends on it."]}],"example":{"title":"A counter with private state","body":"makeCounter returns three functions that share one private variable. Nothing outside can change count except through these functions, and two counters made by separate calls are independent because each call creates a new scope.","code":{"language":"javascript","source":"function makeCounter(start = 0) {
  let count = start;            // private, kept alive by the closures below
  return {
    increment: () => ++count,
    decrement: () => --count,
    value: () => count
  };
}

const a = makeCounter();
const b = makeCounter(10);
a.increment();
a.increment();
b.decrement();
console.log(a.value(), b.value()); // 2 9"}},"use_cases":[{"title":"React hooks","description":"useState, useEffect and custom hooks rely on functions that capture the props and state of the render in which they were created."},{"title":"Event handlers","description":"onClick={() => removeItem(item.id)} closes over item, so each button knows which item it belongs to."},{"title":"Debounce and throttle","description":"Utility functions keep the timer id in a closure so repeated calls can cancel the previous timer."},{"title":"Module privacy","description":"Factory functions and modules hide internal variables and expose only a small public API."},{"title":"Memoisation","description":"A memoize helper stores its cache in a closure so that results persist between calls without a global variable."}],"demonstration":{"description":"The classic interview puzzle: the same loop prints different results with var and let. With var there is one shared binding that has reached 3 when the timers fire; with let each iteration gets a fresh binding that each callback closes over.","code":{"language":"javascript","source":"for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log('var', i), 0);
}
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log('let', j), 0);
}"},"output":"var 3
var 3
var 3
let 0
