let 2"},"takeaways":["JavaScript scope is lexical: decided by where code is written, not where it runs.","Prefer const, use let when reassigning, and avoid var in modern code.","A closure keeps a live reference to outer variables, not a copy of their values.","Every React render creates new closures that see that render's props and state.","Stale closures are fixed with functional state updates, correct dependencies or refs.","Long-lived closures can leak memory, so always clean up listeners and timers."],"interview":[{"question":"What is a closure in JavaScript?","answer":"A closure is a function bundled with references to the variables in the scope where it was defined. It can read and update those variables even after the outer function has returned.","explanation":"A strong answer adds a small example (a counter factory) and stresses that the closure holds a live reference, not a snapshot copy."},{"question":"What is the difference between var, let and const?","answer":"var is function-scoped and hoisted as undefined; let and const are block-scoped and in the temporal dead zone until declared. const prevents reassignment of the binding but does not make objects immutable.","explanation":"Interviewers often follow up with 'can you push to a const array?' (yes), so mention that const is about the binding, not the value."},{"question":"Why does a for loop with var and setTimeout print the same number three times?","answer":"var creates a single function-scoped variable shared by all iterations. The callbacks run after the loop ends, when the shared variable already holds its final value. let creates a new binding per iteration, so each callback sees its own value.","explanation":"Mention the event loop: timers run only after the synchronous loop completes. Offering the let fix and the older IIFE fix shows depth."},{"question":"What is a stale closure in React and how do you fix it?","answer":"It is a callback that captured state or props from an earlier render and therefore reads an outdated value, for example setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1)) inside an effect with an empty dependency array. Fix it with a functional update (setCount(c => c + 1)), by listing the value in the dependency array, or by reading the latest value from a ref.","explanation":"This connects a JavaScript fundamental to real React bugs, which is exactly what front-end interviewers want to see."},{"question":"Can closures cause memory leaks? Give an example.","answer":"Yes. If a closure that references a large object is stored somewhere long-lived, such as a window event listener or an interval that is never cleared, the object cannot be garbage-collected. Removing listeners and clearing timers in cleanup functions prevents it.","explanation":"Link the answer to useEffect cleanup, which you will study on Day 11."}],"practice":{"kind":"coding","title":"Counter factory with private state","difficulty":"Basic","objective":"Use closures to give each counter its own private value, start and step, exactly like independent component instances keep independent state.","statement":"Implement makeCounter(start, step) that returns inc, dec, reset and get functions sharing a private value. Then process commands. 'create <name> <start> <step>' creates a counter (print 'error: <name> exists' if the name is taken). 'inc <name>' adds step, 'dec <name>' subtracts step, 'reset <name>' returns to start, and 'get <name>' prints '<name>=<value>'. Any command on an unknown counter prints 'error: <name> not found'.","language":"javascript","input_format":"First line: N, the number of commands. Next N lines: one command each.","output_format":"One line for every get command and every error, in order.","example":{"input":"6
create a 0 1
create b 10 5
inc a
inc a
dec b
get a
","output":"a=2"},"constraints":["1 <= N <= 1000.","start and step are integers (step may be negative).","Names are single words without spaces."],"starter":"function makeCounter(start, step) {
  // TODO: keep a private value and return { inc, dec, reset, get }
}

const lines = stdin.trim().split('\n');
const n = parseInt(lines[0], 10);
const counters = new Map();
const out = [];
// TODO: process each command
console.log(out.join('\n'));
","solution":"function makeCounter(start, step) {
  let value = start;
  return {
    inc: () => { value += step; },
    dec: () => { value -= step; },
    reset: () => { value = start; },
    get: () => value
  };
}

const lines = stdin.trim().split('\n');
const n = parseInt(lines[0], 10);
const counters = new Map();
const out = [];
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  const [cmd, name, a, b] = lines[i].trim().split(/\s+/);
  if (cmd === 'create') {
    if (counters.has(name)) { out.push('error: ' + name + ' exists'); continue; }
    counters.set(name, makeCounter(Number(a), Number(b)));
    continue;
  }
  const counter = counters.get(name);
  if (!counter) { out.push('error: ' + name + ' not found'); continue; }
  if (cmd === 'get') out.push(name + '=' + counter.get());
  else counter[cmd]();
}
console.log(out.join('\n'));
","explanation":["makeCounter declares value with let; the four returned arrow functions close over it, so it stays private and alive.","Each create call runs makeCounter again, producing a fresh scope, so counters never share values.","reset works because start is also captured by the closure.","The command loop looks up the counter by name and calls the matching method, printing only for get and errors."],"tests":[{"input":"6
